  <ui-tests>
    <ui-test>Regarding mode: given a mocked `by-regarding` response with ≥2 threads across ≥2 months, the component renders each thread as a collapsible group showing the thread name + message count; expanding a group reveals its email+chat messages interleaved in chronological order — no console errors.</ui-test>
    <ui-test>Dark mode (ADR-021): the group headers, count badges, expand chevrons, and expanded rows render correctly under a dark `FluentProvider` (semantic tokens; no hardcoded colors).</ui-test>
    <ui-test>Access parity (NFR-03): when the mocked `by-regarding` response omits a private/internal-only message (server already filtered it), the component shows exactly the returned set — it performs NO client-side hiding or privacy re-derivation.</ui-test>
    <ui-test>Thread-id mode regression: rendering the component in the original single-thread mode behaves exactly as R1 (rows interleaved, channel-badged, compose box present) — the new mode did not regress it.</ui-test>
    <ui-test>Expand/collapse: toggling a group updates `aria-expanded` and shows/hides that thread's timeline; other groups are unaffected.</ui-test>
  </ui-tests>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">The `CommunicationTimeline` component accepts a regarding mode taking `(entityType, id)` and renders the record's threads as collapsible groups, each header showing the thread `sprk_name` + a message count.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Expanding a group renders that thread's email + chat messages interleaved in chronological order, reusing the R1 `buildTimeline`/`MessageRow`/`ChannelBadge` engine (grep confirms reuse, not a forked copy).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Regarding mode calls `GET /api/communications/by-regarding/{entityType}/{id}` (task 010) via the injected `authenticatedFetch` through a new `communicationTimelineApi.readByRegarding` wrapper.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The component performs NO client-side access/privacy filtering — it renders exactly the access-filtered set the endpoint returns (NFR-03).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">The existing thread-id mode is unchanged (its tests still pass); the regarding mode is additive.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">No `@spaarke/auth` import and no `@azure/communication-*` import anywhere under `CommunicationTimeline/**` or `communicationTimelineApi.ts` (grep-verified; ADR-028 + NFR-04).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Only `@fluentui/react-components` + `makeStyles` + `tokens` used for styling; no hardcoded colors; dark mode passes through the host `FluentProvider` (ADR-021).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">`npm run build` + `npm run lint` clean in `@spaarke/ui-components`; the shared-lib barrel still exports `CommunicationTimeline` and any new regarding-mode types.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>
    Task 021 packages this component (in regarding mode) as a form-bound PCF and deploys it; task 022 places that PCF on all 11 regarding-family forms. Keep the regarding-mode props prop-driven `(entityType, id, authenticatedFetch, bffBaseUrl)` so the PCF can wire them from the host record context.

    The `by-regarding` endpoint (task 010) is entity-set-agnostic and access-filtered server-side — the component trusts it fully (NFR-03). Do NOT reintroduce membership-union or any client-side privacy logic (retired 2026-07-16; `../messaging-communication-app-r1/notes/access-model-decision.md`).

    Polling: R2's live-update model stays BFF-polling (NFR-06 — no notification-spine dependency). Whether the regarding-mode grouped view polls (and at what cadence) is a design decision — reuse the R1 `useThreadPoll` cadence pattern if polling the grouped view; state the choice in notes. Do NOT add any push/fan-out mechanism.
  </notes>

